# OTP manual page

.TH OTP 1 "2026-03-18" "OTP v1.0.4" "User Commands"

.SH NAME
otp \- simple one‑time pad encryption/decryption utility

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B otp
[options] <keyfile>
.br
.B otp
[-h | --help]
.br
.B otp
[-nk | --new-key-pair] <size_in_MB> <part_a_name> <part_b_name>

.SH DESCRIPTION
This program takes data from standard input, XORs it with a key file, and writes the result to standard output.  After processing it writes a new file containing the part of the key file that was not used, ending with ``.next``.  This ensures the key cannot be reused.  The key file must be a binary file containing random data; after each use the unused portion is moved to a new file for the next run.

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Display this help message and exit.

.TP
.B \-nk, \-\-new-key-pair
Generate a new key pair.  The program expects two key files for encryption and decryption.  The key material is read from standard input: the first half is written to an encryption file named ``encryption_<part_a_name>.txt``; the second half to ``decryption_<part_a_name>.txt``; and so on for ``part_b_name``.  The first argument is the size in megabytes (e.g., ``5`` for 5 MiB).  Example: cat /dev/urandom | otp --new-key-pair 5 partA partB

.SH USAGE
To encrypt:
.PP
.B echo "plain text" | otp key.txt > cipher.txt
.PP
To decrypt:
.PP
.B cat cipher.txt | otp key.txt > plain.txt
.PP
After each run, the file ``key.txt.next`` will contain the unused key bytes.

.SH NEXT KEY
Every time you run the command it will create a new file with the same name as the key file ending with ``.next``.

.SH NEW KEY PAIR GENERATION
Use the \fB-nk\fP or \fB--new-key-pair\fP flag to generate a new key pair from a source of randomness.
Example:
.B cat /dev/urandom | otp --new-key-pair 1 alice bob

.SH TRUE RANDOM KEY GENERATOR
Only a true random key makes this algorithm unbreakable. To generate a true random key consider
.B https://www.crowdsupply.com/leetronics/infinite-noise-trng

.SH SEE ALSO
openssl(1), gpg(1)

